<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Fulk Nerra</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 970–<a href="/years/1040">1040</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as Fulk III of Anjou</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Fulk Nerra, Fulk III, Count of Anjou from 987 to 1040, was one of the most aggressive princes of medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. He built a chain of stone castles, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/langeais">Langeais</a> and Loches, that carried Angevin power into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/touraine">Touraine</a> and against the counts of Blois. Notoriously violent, he also made several pilgrimages to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> and founded abbeys.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/fulk-nerra.jpg" alt="Fulk Nerra" width="320" height="314" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Georges de Manteyer · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sceau_de_Foulques_Nerra.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Fulk III, known as Fulk the Black or Fulk Nerra, was born around 970, the son of Geoffrey I of Anjou, also called Geoffrey Grisegonelle, and Adele of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meaux">Meaux</a>. He was a natural horseman and fearsome warrior with a keen sense of strategy, and he sided with the Capetians against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carolingian-empire">Carolingians</a>. His county seat was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angers">Angers</a>, and his bitter enemy was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odo-ii-of-blois">Odo II of Blois</a>, his neighbour to the east along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/loire">Loire</a>; the two traded towns, followers and insults throughout their lives.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Fulk is regarded as one of the first great builders of medieval castles, and is estimated to have constructed about a hundred, as well as abbeys, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/loire">Loire</a> valley. His first castle, at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/langeais">Langeais</a>, began as a wooden tower and was later replaced in stone with a donjon. It stood in the territory of Odo I of Blois, and they fought over it in 994, but Odo I died suddenly and his son did not manage to evict Fulk. He went on building in a slow encirclement of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tours">Tours</a>: Montbazon, Montrésor, Mirebeau, Montrichard, Loches and in 1016 Montboyau.</p><p>In June 992 he won his first victory, at Conquereuil, over Conan I, Duke of Brittany, who was killed. The biggest battle against Odo II was fought at Pontlevoy in July 1016. Fulk's smaller force attacked Odo's 10,000 men from behind and was routed, but Odo, thinking the battle won, went swimming in the Cher, and reinforcements led by Count Herbert Wake-Dog of Maine arrived and routed his surprised men.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Fulk made four pilgrimages to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>. The first, in 1003, was penitential, and the second, in 1008, was imposed by the king as punishment after Fulk ordered the murder of an enemy. For the third, in 1035 with Robert I, Duke of Normandy, and the fourth, in 1038, he felt a duty to protect pilgrims and provided armed security along the route. He died at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/metz">Metz</a> in 1040 on his return and was buried at his monastery at Beaulieu.</p><p>Some sources call him devout, noting that he built or endowed abbeys; others see this as an anxious effort to ensure his salvation after a life of ruthless violence against enemies and his own family. His wife Elisabeth died by burning on a charge of adultery, which one account says he trumped up. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geoffrey-ii-count-of-anjou">Geoffrey Martel</a>, by his second wife Hildegarde, succeeded him.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Fulk III of Anjou” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His successors were challenged by the counts of Rennes, whose Conan I claimed the ducal title and died fighting <b>Fulk Nerra of Anjou</b> at the Battle of Conquereuil in 992.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-brittany">Duchy of Brittany · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The counts ruled from Angers with a dense network of stone castles, many built by <b>Fulk Nerra</b> (987–1040), which let them push into Touraine and Maine.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-anjou">County of Anjou</a></li><li><blockquote>Count Herbert I, known as Wakedog, defended it against <b>Fulk Nerra</b> of Anjou in the early 11th century.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-maine">County of Maine</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/metz">Metz</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=49.12,6.18&amp;year=1015" aria-label="Metz on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the place of death of Fulk Nerra"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5345 5909h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5345 5909h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=49.12,6.18&amp;year=1015">What was here in 1015? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
